1
00:00:01,280 --> 00:00:03,221
There's a famous quote about bees.

2
00:00:03,551 --> 00:00:05,851
If the bee disappeared from the face of the Earth,

3
00:00:06,091 --> 00:00:08,784
humans would have only four years left to live.

4
00:00:09,114 --> 00:00:11,659
It's usually signed: Albert Einstein.

5
00:00:12,009 --> 00:00:13,389
There's just one problem.

6
00:00:13,479 --> 00:00:15,111
Einstein never said it.

7
00:00:15,851 --> 00:00:19,028
The earliest version anyone has found appeared

8
00:00:19,048 --> 00:00:21,371
in a French magazine in 1965.

9
00:00:21,651 --> 00:00:23,357
Ten years after he died.

10
00:00:23,737 --> 00:00:25,386
But the question behind the fake

11
00:00:25,406 --> 00:00:26,727
quote is a real one.

12
00:00:26,977 --> 00:00:29,752
What would actually happen if every bee disappeared?

13
00:00:30,372 --> 00:00:31,752
The true answer is less like

14
00:00:31,772 --> 00:00:33,062
the end of the world,

15
00:00:33,242 --> 00:00:36,085
and more like a very strange trip to the supermarket.

16
00:00:41,325 --> 00:00:42,225
Chapter one.

17
00:00:42,245 --> 00:00:43,753
What a bee actually does.

18
00:00:44,453 --> 00:00:45,863
Here's the strange part:

19
00:00:45,923 --> 00:00:48,173
bees have no idea they're feeding you.

20
00:00:48,503 --> 00:00:50,713
A bee doesn't visit flowers to help us.

21
00:00:50,833 --> 00:00:51,803
She's shopping.

22
00:00:52,003 --> 00:00:53,143
Nectar for energy,

23
00:00:53,163 --> 00:00:54,782
and pollen to feed her young.

24
00:00:55,112 --> 00:00:56,542
But pollen is sticky,

25
00:00:56,582 --> 00:00:57,842
and bees are fuzzy.

26
00:00:58,112 --> 00:00:59,232
So on every visit,

27
00:00:59,252 --> 00:01:00,845
a little golden dust from one

28
00:01:00,865 --> 00:01:02,841
flower rides along to the next.

29
00:01:03,171 --> 00:01:04,451
That's pollination.

30
00:01:04,741 --> 00:01:07,361
When pollen reaches a flower of the same kind,

31
00:01:07,431 --> 00:01:09,401
the flower can swell into a fruit,

32
00:01:09,421 --> 00:01:10,837
with seeds inside.

33
00:01:11,177 --> 00:01:15,187
No delivery, and many plants give smaller fruit, misshapen fruit,

34
00:01:15,217 --> 00:01:16,549
or no fruit at all.

35
00:01:16,879 --> 00:01:18,799
And bees are not one animal.

36
00:01:19,159 --> 00:01:22,219
There are about twenty thousand species of wild bees,

37
00:01:22,319 --> 00:01:24,203
from big fuzzy bumblebees to bees

38
00:01:24,223 --> 00:01:26,209
smaller than a grain of rice.

39
00:01:26,589 --> 00:01:28,011
The honeybee on your jar of

40
00:01:28,031 --> 00:01:29,745
honey is just one of them.

41
00:01:30,915 --> 00:01:31,775
Chapter two.

42
00:01:31,795 --> 00:01:33,321
The supermarket test.

43
00:01:34,011 --> 00:01:35,651
If every bee vanished tonight,

44
00:01:35,751 --> 00:01:38,341
what would be missing from your shopping basket tomorrow?

45
00:01:38,661 --> 00:01:39,791
Less than you'd think,

46
00:01:39,811 --> 00:01:41,155
and more than you'd like.

47
00:01:41,485 --> 00:01:43,753
Imagine walking into a supermarket on the

48
00:01:43,773 --> 00:01:46,078
morning after the last bee is gone.

49
00:01:46,428 --> 00:01:47,873
Scientists who studied the world's leading food crops found

50
00:01:47,893 --> 00:01:49,478
that eighty-seven of them depend on animal pollination,

51
00:01:49,628 --> 00:01:54,128
at least partly.

52
00:01:54,488 --> 00:01:56,100
Only twenty-eight don't.

53
00:01:56,420 --> 00:01:57,630
Counted by type,

54
00:01:57,750 --> 00:01:59,389
more than three quarters of the

55
00:01:59,409 --> 00:02:01,580
world's food crops rely on pollinators,

56
00:02:01,650 --> 00:02:02,760
at least partly.

57
00:02:03,120 --> 00:02:03,870
Together,

58
00:02:03,970 --> 00:02:05,583
they're worth up to five hundred

59
00:02:05,603 --> 00:02:07,935
and seventy-seven billion dollars a year.

60
00:02:08,285 --> 00:02:10,134
So start in the fruit aisle.

61
00:02:10,154 --> 00:02:12,662
Apples, pears, cherries, melons, blueberries.

62
00:02:12,682 --> 00:02:13,596
Fewer of them,

63
00:02:13,616 --> 00:02:15,525
and much more expensive.

64
00:02:15,875 --> 00:02:17,055
Then vegetables.

65
00:02:17,265 --> 00:02:18,955
Pumpkins, cucumbers,

66
00:02:18,975 --> 00:02:20,606
and even the seeds farmers need

67
00:02:20,626 --> 00:02:22,347
to grow carrots and onions.

68
00:02:22,707 --> 00:02:24,107
Nuts take a heavy hit,

69
00:02:24,127 --> 00:02:25,427
almonds most of all.

70
00:02:25,667 --> 00:02:27,911
And your coffee gets a little harder to grow.

71
00:02:28,261 --> 00:02:30,786
But now, look at the other side of the store.

72
00:02:31,516 --> 00:02:33,322
Bread, rice, pasta, cornflakes.

73
00:02:33,342 --> 00:02:36,576
Wheat, rice and maize are pollinated by the wind.

74
00:02:36,596 --> 00:02:38,883
They don't need a single bee.

75
00:02:39,233 --> 00:02:44,143
That's why, by sheer weight,

76
00:02:44,163 --> 00:02:44,865
about sixty percent of the world's food comes

77
00:02:44,885 --> 00:02:45,803
from crops that don't need animals at all.

78
00:02:46,233 --> 00:02:48,905
Thirty-five percent comes from crops that do.

79
00:02:49,255 --> 00:02:51,085
So we wouldn't run out of calories.

80
00:02:51,215 --> 00:02:52,505
We'd run out of colour.

81
00:02:52,765 --> 00:02:54,260
The foods that fade away are

82
00:02:54,280 --> 00:02:56,111
the ones packed with vitamins.

83
00:02:57,291 --> 00:02:58,121
Chapter three.

84
00:02:58,141 --> 00:02:59,345
The human bees.

85
00:03:00,025 --> 00:03:02,175
Could people do a bee's job by hand?

86
00:03:02,355 --> 00:03:03,775
In one valley in China,

87
00:03:03,795 --> 00:03:04,704
they had to.

88
00:03:05,024 --> 00:03:06,434
To see what that looks like,

89
00:03:06,544 --> 00:03:09,307
travel to the mountains of Sichuan, in China.

90
00:03:09,637 --> 00:03:10,947
In Maoxian County,

91
00:03:10,997 --> 00:03:13,357
apple and pear trees bloom every spring.

92
00:03:13,647 --> 00:03:14,467
But for years,

93
00:03:14,487 --> 00:03:16,044
not enough insects came.

94
00:03:16,454 --> 00:03:20,164
Farmers had sprayed pesticides eight to ten times a season,

95
00:03:20,284 --> 00:03:21,774
for about four decades.

96
00:03:22,114 --> 00:03:23,854
And the forests and grasslands around

97
00:03:23,874 --> 00:03:25,981
the valley were turning into farmland.

98
00:03:26,311 --> 00:03:28,134
So, from the late nineteen-eighties,

99
00:03:28,154 --> 00:03:30,925
people started doing the bees' job themselves.

100
00:03:31,265 --> 00:03:32,735
They collected flowers,

101
00:03:32,755 --> 00:03:34,345
built up a stock of pollen,

102
00:03:34,505 --> 00:03:36,024
and climbed into the trees to

103
00:03:36,044 --> 00:03:37,575
dab it onto the blossoms,

104
00:03:37,595 --> 00:03:38,686
one by one.

105
00:03:39,016 --> 00:03:41,870
A survey in 2001 found that every single

106
00:03:41,890 --> 00:03:44,996
apple in Maoxian was pollinated by hand.

107
00:03:45,336 --> 00:03:46,796
And the clock is brutal.

108
00:03:47,046 --> 00:03:48,841
The flowers have to be pollinated

109
00:03:48,861 --> 00:03:51,029
within about five days of opening.

110
00:03:51,369 --> 00:03:54,203
One person can do five to ten trees a day.

111
00:03:54,553 --> 00:03:56,733
As young workers left for the cities,

112
00:03:56,893 --> 00:03:59,543
the cost of that labour rose about tenfold,

113
00:03:59,653 --> 00:04:02,539
to twelve to nineteen dollars per person, per day.

114
00:04:02,879 --> 00:04:04,649
Many farmers gave up on apples,

115
00:04:04,689 --> 00:04:05,899
and switched to plums,

116
00:04:05,919 --> 00:04:07,569
loquats and walnuts.

117
00:04:07,899 --> 00:04:08,949
It made sense.

118
00:04:09,109 --> 00:04:11,849
Apples were selling for less than twenty cents a kilo.

119
00:04:12,049 --> 00:04:14,093
Plums fetched around two dollars.

120
00:04:14,443 --> 00:04:16,134
Humans can do a bee's job.

121
00:04:16,154 --> 00:04:17,624
Just slowly, expensively,

122
00:04:17,644 --> 00:04:19,165
and not for long.

123
00:04:20,945 --> 00:04:21,776
Chapter four.

124
00:04:21,796 --> 00:04:24,067
One valley, almost every hive.

125
00:04:24,757 --> 00:04:26,381
What if almost every beehive in a

126
00:04:26,401 --> 00:04:28,417
country had to visit the same valley?

127
00:04:28,737 --> 00:04:31,196
In America, it happens every year.

128
00:04:31,556 --> 00:04:33,396
Now look at the opposite approach.

129
00:04:33,436 --> 00:04:34,541
California.

130
00:04:34,881 --> 00:04:36,520
Most of the world's almonds grow

131
00:04:36,540 --> 00:04:38,101
in one long valley there.

132
00:04:38,291 --> 00:04:40,341
And almond orchards need bees.

133
00:04:40,541 --> 00:04:42,643
About two hives for every acre.

134
00:04:42,993 --> 00:04:44,133
So every winter,

135
00:04:44,263 --> 00:04:46,543
beekeepers load their hives onto trucks,

136
00:04:46,633 --> 00:04:49,379
and drive them in from all over the United States.

137
00:04:49,749 --> 00:04:51,689
For the 2024 bloom,

138
00:04:51,819 --> 00:04:55,389
growers needed around two point seven million colonies.

139
00:04:55,749 --> 00:04:57,731
That's roughly as many as the entire

140
00:04:57,751 --> 00:05:00,297
country counted on the first of January.

141
00:05:00,637 --> 00:05:02,081
The average rent was about a

142
00:05:02,101 --> 00:05:04,107
hundred and eighty dollars per hive,

143
00:05:04,207 --> 00:05:05,541
for a few weeks of work.

144
00:05:05,881 --> 00:05:09,989
Almond pollination alone earned beekeepers more than three

145
00:05:10,009 --> 00:05:13,341
hundred and twenty million dollars in 2024.

146
00:05:13,721 --> 00:05:15,316
About eighty percent of all the

147
00:05:15,336 --> 00:05:17,312
pollination money in the country.

148
00:05:17,652 --> 00:05:18,442
One crop.

149
00:05:18,462 --> 00:05:19,352
One valley.

150
00:05:19,462 --> 00:05:21,382
Almost every hive in America.

151
00:05:23,272 --> 00:05:24,172
Chapter five.

152
00:05:24,192 --> 00:05:25,253
The health bill.

153
00:05:25,933 --> 00:05:28,923
Could losing pollinators actually make people sick?

154
00:05:29,163 --> 00:05:31,273
Researchers tried to put a number on it.

155
00:05:31,613 --> 00:05:34,053
So what would losing all of them really cost us?

156
00:05:34,213 --> 00:05:35,093
Not in money.

157
00:05:35,153 --> 00:05:36,025
In health.

158
00:05:36,365 --> 00:05:40,555
In 2015, a team of researchers modelled exactly that:

159
00:05:40,875 --> 00:05:43,555
a world where every animal pollinator is gone.

160
00:05:43,705 --> 00:05:44,875
Not just bees.

161
00:05:44,895 --> 00:05:45,770
All of them.

162
00:05:46,100 --> 00:05:49,773
Global fruit supplies fell by about twenty-three percent.

163
00:05:49,793 --> 00:05:51,216
Vegetables, by sixteen.

164
00:05:51,236 --> 00:05:53,488
Nuts and seeds, by twenty-two.

165
00:05:53,818 --> 00:05:57,358
Seventy-one million more people could become short of vitamin A.

166
00:05:57,708 --> 00:06:00,098
A hundred and seventy-three million more,

167
00:06:00,138 --> 00:06:01,249
short of folate.

168
00:06:01,579 --> 00:06:03,663
And that's on top of the two point two

169
00:06:03,683 --> 00:06:06,812
billion people who already don't get enough vitamin A.

170
00:06:07,152 --> 00:06:09,462
And with fewer fruits and vegetables on the plate,

171
00:06:09,692 --> 00:06:11,482
heart disease, strokes,

172
00:06:11,502 --> 00:06:13,997
diabetes and some cancers would rise.

173
00:06:14,327 --> 00:06:15,181
Their estimate:

174
00:06:15,201 --> 00:06:18,368
about one point four million extra deaths every year.

175
00:06:18,388 --> 00:06:21,267
Almost three percent more deaths, worldwide.

176
00:06:21,597 --> 00:06:22,367
It's a model,

177
00:06:22,387 --> 00:06:23,907
built on big assumptions.

178
00:06:24,047 --> 00:06:25,635
But the direction is clear.

179
00:06:25,995 --> 00:06:27,765
Here's the uncomfortable part.

180
00:06:28,135 --> 00:06:32,021
A follow-up study in 2022 estimated that poor pollination is

181
00:06:32,041 --> 00:06:36,085
already costing three to five percent of the world's fruit,

182
00:06:36,215 --> 00:06:37,919
vegetables and nuts.

183
00:06:38,269 --> 00:06:40,326
That loss is linked to about four hundred

184
00:06:40,346 --> 00:06:42,799
and twenty-seven thousand excess deaths a year.

185
00:06:43,029 --> 00:06:43,999
Not someday.

186
00:06:44,179 --> 00:06:44,869
Now.

187
00:06:46,839 --> 00:06:47,839
Chapter six.

188
00:06:47,859 --> 00:06:49,117
Who else is out there?

189
00:06:49,807 --> 00:06:51,527
Do we really need bees at all?

190
00:06:51,607 --> 00:06:53,430
Some of your favourite foods don't.

191
00:06:53,770 --> 00:06:54,570
But wait.

192
00:06:54,680 --> 00:06:56,370
Our question was about bees.

193
00:06:56,470 --> 00:06:58,780
And bees are not the only pollinators.

194
00:06:59,110 --> 00:07:00,090
Take chocolate.

195
00:07:00,350 --> 00:07:03,920
The cacao tree's tiny flowers are pollinated by midges.

196
00:07:04,150 --> 00:07:06,711
Flies just one to three millimetres long.

197
00:07:07,041 --> 00:07:08,531
So in a world without bees,

198
00:07:08,551 --> 00:07:09,921
chocolate would survive.

199
00:07:10,051 --> 00:07:10,931
Probably.

200
00:07:11,321 --> 00:07:14,946
Flies, beetles, moths and butterflies visit flowers too.

201
00:07:14,966 --> 00:07:16,534
Hummingbirds sip nectar.

202
00:07:16,554 --> 00:07:17,474
And at night,

203
00:07:17,494 --> 00:07:18,886
bats pollinate agave,

204
00:07:18,906 --> 00:07:20,953
the plant behind tequila.

205
00:07:21,283 --> 00:07:23,783
But none of them do the job quite like bees.

206
00:07:23,963 --> 00:07:25,933
Bees feed their young on pollen.

207
00:07:26,083 --> 00:07:28,223
Their whole bodies are built to collect it.

208
00:07:28,573 --> 00:07:30,636
Losing them wouldn't end life on Earth.

209
00:07:30,656 --> 00:07:33,423
It would make it smaller, plainer, and harder.

210
00:07:35,203 --> 00:07:36,193
Chapter seven.

211
00:07:36,213 --> 00:07:37,386
The real news.

212
00:07:38,066 --> 00:07:39,176
So, should we panic?

213
00:07:39,196 --> 00:07:39,667
Not yet.

214
00:07:39,687 --> 00:07:41,337
But the real story is about

215
00:07:41,357 --> 00:07:43,470
the bees you've never heard of.

216
00:07:43,820 --> 00:07:45,970
Here's a number that is easy to miss.

217
00:07:46,320 --> 00:07:47,830
In the last fifty years,

218
00:07:47,920 --> 00:07:50,565
the amount of food we grow from pollinator-dependent

219
00:07:50,585 --> 00:07:53,100
crops has gone up by three hundred percent.

220
00:07:53,340 --> 00:07:54,815
We need them more than ever.

221
00:07:55,165 --> 00:07:56,704
So, are bees disappearing?

222
00:07:56,724 --> 00:07:57,779
Not all at once.

223
00:07:57,799 --> 00:07:59,875
Honeybees are kept like livestock,

224
00:07:59,895 --> 00:08:03,066
and beekeepers replace the colonies they lose.

225
00:08:03,406 --> 00:08:05,646
The real worry is the wild ones.

226
00:08:05,976 --> 00:08:07,736
Where scientists have assessed them,

227
00:08:07,846 --> 00:08:11,166
more than forty percent of invertebrate pollinator species,

228
00:08:11,236 --> 00:08:14,475
mostly bees and butterflies, face extinction.

229
00:08:14,795 --> 00:08:17,695
The causes are the ones Maoxian learned the hard way.

230
00:08:18,025 --> 00:08:20,715
Lost habitat, heavy pesticide use,

231
00:08:20,755 --> 00:08:22,214
and a changing climate.

232
00:08:22,544 --> 00:08:24,694
And the fixes are surprisingly simple.

233
00:08:24,864 --> 00:08:27,194
Strips of wildflowers along the fields.

234
00:08:27,344 --> 00:08:29,144
Fewer, smarter sprays.

235
00:08:29,364 --> 00:08:31,745
A corner of the garden left a little wild.

236
00:08:32,875 --> 00:08:35,175
Einstein never warned us about bees.

237
00:08:35,355 --> 00:08:37,511
But the quote sticks around for a reason.

238
00:08:37,851 --> 00:08:39,291
So many of the apples,

239
00:08:39,431 --> 00:08:41,226
almonds and bright colours on your

240
00:08:41,246 --> 00:08:43,331
plate began with a tiny delivery.

241
00:08:44,181 --> 00:08:45,244
This is Whyverse.

242
00:08:45,264 --> 00:08:47,389
Big questions, visual answers.

243
00:08:47,719 --> 00:08:48,909
If you enjoyed this,

244
00:08:48,929 --> 00:08:51,619
give the video a like and subscribe to Whyverse.

245
00:08:51,889 --> 00:08:53,289
And for shorter stories,

246
00:08:53,399 --> 00:08:55,475
find us on Instagram and TikTok.

